[0m[0m
The program is small (a [38;5;10mstatic_sum[0m accumulator plus argument parsing), so I did the translation directly rather than delegating — dispatching sub-agents would have cost more than it saved, and there was no fan-out to parallelize.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three behaviors in the C needed deliberate preservation rather than "correct" Rust:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mint stride = strtol(argv[1], &end, 10)[0m narrows a [38;5;10mlong[0m to [38;5;10mint[0m. So [38;5;10m4294967296[0m truncates to [38;5;10m0[0m and [38;5;10m9223372036854775808[0m saturates to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m then truncates to [38;5;10m-1[0m. I reimplemented [38;5;10mstrtol[0m over raw argv bytes (C-locale whitespace skip, optional sign, base-10 digits, [38;5;10mERANGE[0m clamping to [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m) and applied [38;5;10mas i32[0m.[0m[0m
- The validity check is [38;5;10mend == argv[1][0m, not "consumed the whole string." [38;5;10m"42abc"[0m and [38;5;10m"0x10"[0m are accepted; [38;5;10m"+"[0m, [38;5;10m"."[0m, and [38;5;10m""[0m are rejected. My port returns a consumed-byte count and tests it against 0.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mi * stride[0m and [38;5;10msum += update[0m are signed [38;5;10mint[0m overflow, which is UB in C but wraps on the usual targets. I used [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m so large strides produce the same wrapped values gcc emits instead of panicking in debug.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Everything is safe Rust. The function-local [38;5;10mstatic int sum[0m became a [38;5;10mthread_local! { Cell<i32> }[0m, which reproduces process-lifetime accumulator semantics for this single-threaded program without [38;5;10munsafe[0m. Argv is read as raw bytes via [38;5;10mOsStrExt[0m so non-UTF-8 arguments parse exactly as [38;5;10mstrtol[0m would — verified against inputs like [38;5;10m$'\xff\xfe123'[0m and [38;5;10m$'12\xff34'[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two notes on scope. The stdin-reading requirement in your instructions doesn't apply here: this program reads no stdin, only [38;5;10margv[0m, so there was nothing to match. And stdout buffering differs in timing — C block-buffers to a pipe while Rust line-buffers — but since all output is newline-terminated and flushed before exit, the resulting bytes are identical; I did not attempt to mimic the buffering itself.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One caveat on the verification: I compared against [38;5;10mgcc -O2[0m on this host. The wrapping-overflow and long-to-int-truncation cases are UB or implementation-defined in C, so those specific outputs are what this toolchain and target produce, not guarantees of the C standard. A different compiler or optimization level could in principle differ on the overflow cases.[0m[0m
